```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                      fig.path = "figures/imputation-",
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5, dpi = 80,
                      out.width = "100%")
```

Traits are rarely measured on everyone. Dropping the individuals with a gap
throws away the traits that *were* measured on them, and with relatedness it is
unnecessary: a missing value is informed by every relative's observed traits.
The opossum data ship that way (11 of the animals with a recorded sex and age
have none of these four traits measured), so the model is fitted with the gaps in place and `imputed_data()` fills them from
the relatives' records.

```{r packages, message = FALSE}
library(stanimal)
library(ggplot2)
```

## Data, with the gaps already in it

Four distances spanning the skull from the nasal suture to the basicranium,
positively correlated with each other (0.39 to 0.78). That correlation is what
lets a gap borrow from the other traits of the same animal, on top of what its
relatives say.

```{r data}
traits <- c("IS_NSL", "NA_BR", "ZI_ZYGO", "BA_EAM")
dat <- opossum
dat[traits] <- log(dat[traits])   # morphometrics are fitted on the log scale
```

`animal_design()` drops the rows with a missing predictor (age), but keeps the
rows whose response has gaps.

```{r design}
design <- suppressWarnings(
  animal_design(cbind(IS_NSL, NA_BR, ZI_ZYGO, BA_EAM) ~ sex + age, data = dat))
Y <- design$Y
A <- opossum_grm[rownames(Y), rownames(Y)]
c(missing_entries = sum(is.na(Y)), entries = length(Y),
  incomplete_animals = sum(!complete.cases(Y)),
  complete_animals = sum(complete.cases(Y)))
```

## Fit with the gaps in place

Nothing is dropped: the gaps become parameters of the model, with a flat prior,
so the posterior over `G`, `E` and `beta` is the exact marginal over the observed
data.

```{r fit, message = FALSE}
fit <- lmm_animal(Y, design$X, A, lkj_prior = 2,
                  iter = 800, warmup = 400, chains = 2,
                  refresh = 0, seed = 1)
fit$summary(c("h2", "corrG"))
```

## The completed data

`imputed_data()` returns individuals by traits by draws, with the gaps filled on
the response scale. These are multiple imputations: do not collapse them to one
filled-in matrix and treat it as data.

```{r imputed}
imp <- imputed_data(fit, ndraws = 500, seed = 2)
imp
dim(imp$Y)
```

What the model believes about each gap:

```{r plot, fig.height = 6}
gaps <- imp$gaps
intervals <- t(vapply(seq_len(nrow(gaps)), function(i) {
  v <- imp$Y[gaps$individual[i], gaps$trait[i], ]
  c(mean = mean(v), lo = unname(quantile(v, 0.025)),
    hi = unname(quantile(v, 0.975)))
}, c(mean = 0, lo = 0, hi = 0)))
plot_dat <- data.frame(
  gap = sprintf("%s / %s", gaps$individual_label, gaps$trait_label),
  trait = gaps$trait_label,
  mean = intervals[, "mean"], lo = intervals[, "lo"], hi = intervals[, "hi"])
plot_dat$gap <- factor(plot_dat$gap,
                       levels = rev(plot_dat$gap[order(plot_dat$trait)]))

ggplot(plot_dat, aes(x = mean, y = gap)) +
  geom_pointrange(aes(xmin = lo, xmax = hi), colour = "grey30", size = 0.2) +
  facet_wrap(~trait, scales = "free", nrow = 1) +
  labs(x = "log distance", y = NULL,
       title = "Imputed values for the animals with missing measurements",
       subtitle = "posterior mean and 95% interval for each missing value") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(axis.text.y = element_blank())
```
